<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.Views.Settings.AppearanceCard"
             FontSize="{DynamicResource FontSizeBody}">
  <!-- ══ 外观 — four label-and-control rows on the DLU grid ═══════════════════════════════════
       ROUND 8, TASK 6 REPLACED THE ROW GRAMMAR. Until this task every settings row was a
       Border Classes="tm-card" wrapping an Auto,*,Auto Grid: a 16 DIP icon, a title line over a
       60 %-ink subtitle line, and the control trailing. That is Windows 11 Settings' row card, and
       Windows 7 has no such object — a property page is a LABEL AND A CONTROL on vis-layout's
       ladder. So the icon, the card Border and the subtitle are gone, together with the twelve
       Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml rules that carried them, and what is left is a two-column Grid.

       THE COLUMN TEMPLATE IS "Auto,*" AND IT IS WRITTEN LITERALLY IN EVERY CARD, for the reason
       SettingsRowTests has always recorded: Grid has no ColumnDefinitionsProperty, so a Setter
       cannot carry it and only a source test can. Column 0 holds the label, column 1 the control.

       ── THE FontSize ON THIS ROOT IS THE SETTINGS SURFACE'S BODY SIZE, AND IT IS A FIX ─────────
       Every one of the six cards states it, and the review of Task 6's first commit (7e9bee6) is
       why. Deleting TextBlock.tm-card-title and TextBlock.tm-card-sub deleted the ONLY statement
       this surface had of its own body size — both carried FontSize="{DynamicResource
       FontSizeBody}" — and nothing replaced it, so the bare TextBlocks fell back to what they
       inherit. MEASURED, by UIA at 150 %, on that build's captures and again on this one — same
       method, same scale, element bounds in physical px:

         每列个数 (外观)      83 x 30  ->  71 x 25       更新状态： (更新)   105 x 29  ->  89 x 25
         固定到任务栏 (行为)  126 x 29  ->  108 x 25      位置： (文件夹)      53 x 25  ->  53 x 25

       The left column is 14 DIP and the right is 12: at 150 % a CJK glyph advances one em, so six
       characters are 6 x 12 x 1.5 = 108 exactly. 位置： did not move BECAUSE IT NEVER LEFT 12, which
       is the whole shape of the defect — one dialog with two body sizes, and on 行为 a label one
       step LARGER than the button beside it. Every body line box on the surface is 24-26 px now
       (the ±1 is UIA rounding on a wrapping block) and only the four group heads on 文件夹 are 31.

       THE MECHANISM, decompiled rather than assumed (ilspycmd over
       Avalonia.Themes.Fluent 12.1.0): FluentTheme declares a ControlTheme whose TargetType is
       Window with a Setter on TemplatedControl.FontSizeProperty bound to
       {DynamicResource ControlContentThemeFontSize}, and its own resource dictionary adds that key
       as 14.0. Themes/Aero/AVMAP/Metrics.axaml's change #2 DELETED upstream's re-point of that key
       onto AeroFontSizeNormal (12) and Themes/Shell/Tokens.axaml does not redefine it, so an
       Avalonia Window in this app is 14 unless it says otherwise — MainWindow says 14 on purpose
       (its own type ramp), and the two dialogs said nothing. Nearly every vendored Aero ControlTheme
       sets 12 on the control it themes, which is why the themed controls never moved, and why 文件夹
       alone stayed at 12: its content sits inside HeaderedContentControl.axaml's group box, which
       sets FontSize AeroFontSizeNormal (:68). What was left over is the set the design's §1 names —
       "Nothing themes a Border, a TextBlock, a StackPanel, an Image or a Panel" — PLUS the one
       vendored theme that is the exception to "nearly every": CheckBox.axaml sets no FontSize at
       all (grepped — the word does not appear in the file), so the five self-labelling boxes and
       ToolsCard's code-built
       per-tool list drew their labels at 14 too.

       THE ROOT CAUSE IS ONE KEY AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED HERE. Re-pointing
       ControlContentThemeFontSize onto AeroFontSizeNormal in Tokens.axaml — the file that already
       redefines three FluentTheme keys for exactly this reason — would fix this surface AND
       Dialogs/MessageBoxWindow and every Fluent-templated popup in one line. It is not done in a
       review round because it moves type on surfaces no task owns and nobody has measured, and
       MessageBoxWindow's own remarks carry a size record that would go stale unread. Recorded here
       so the next task that owns the message box has the finding rather than the symptom.

       WHY THE CARD ROOT AND NOT THE TWO WINDOWS. The card is the unit that gets re-hosted — Task 4
       moved AboutCard out of a tab and into a window of its own — so the size travels with the
       thing that needs it. A UserControl's FontSize is TextElement.FontSize, which INHERITS, and an
       inherited value is the lowest priority Avalonia has: a ControlTheme Setter still wins, so
       this can never fight the vendored themes (they set the same 12 anyway) and it reaches only
       the primitives nothing themes. TextBlock.tm-group-head's FontSizeGroupHead (15) likewise
       still wins on 文件夹's four heads. Tokens.axaml states the rule this restores: "a Windows 7
       property sheet uses ONE body size and gets its hierarchy from colour and weight."

       EVERY NUMBER BELOW IS PUBLISHED by
       https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/uxguide/vis-layout (relative pixels, 9 pt
       Segoe UI at 96 dpi): ColumnSpacing 5 is "label to control"; the 7 each row carries as its own
       top Margin is "Between related controls — 4 / 7"; the 11 on this Grid is "Dialog box margins
       — 7 DLU / 11 relative pixels on all sides", which the group box used to supply as its Padding
       and which the property page has to supply itself now that five of the six boxes are gone
       (PUBLISHED, ctrl-group-boxes: "Are all the controls inside the group? If so, indicate the
       relationship on the larger surface, such as the parent dialog box or page." — the tab IS that
       larger surface, and OptionsWindow.ApplyText writes 外观 onto its TabItem).

       THE RHYTHM IS PER-ROW Margin RATHER THAN Grid.RowSpacing, and that is deliberate. RowSpacing
       is charged between row definitions even when a row's only child is IsVisible="False", which
       would leave dead space on the two cards that have such a row (BehaviorCard's portable-only
       help line, UpdatesCard's ProgressBar); a collapsed child contributes no margin at all, because
       Layoutable.MeasureCore returns an empty size for it before the margin is ever added.

       THE SUBTITLES ARE GONE AND THE STRINGS WENT WITH THEM. 语言 / 外观 / 字体 each had one, and
       each said what its own label already said ("应用界面显示所使用的语言" under 语言). AppLanguage's
       LanguageCardSub, AppearanceCardSub and FontCardSub are deleted rather than orphaned. 每列个数's
       is the one that survives, as the static line below the spinner: it describes a BEHAVIOUR the
       label cannot ("超出后另起一列"), not a restatement of the label.

       EACH CONTROL TAKES ITS ACCESSIBLE NAME FROM ITS OWN LABEL through
       AutomationProperties.LabeledBy — Avalonia's ControlAutomationPeer.GetNameCore falls back to
       the LabeledBy peer's name when AutomationProperties.Name is unset. That is a name GAINED, not
       preserved: on the card grammar these four controls had no accessible name at all (a ComboBox
       peer has no fallback and the title beside it was an unrelated TextBlock), and only the four
       check boxes on 行为 were wired this way. -->
  <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" RowDefinitions="Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto,Auto"
        ColumnSpacing="5" Margin="11">

    <TextBlock Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="LanguageLabel" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
    <ComboBox Grid.Row="0" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="LanguageCombo" Width="220"
              HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center"
              AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #LanguageLabel}"
              SelectionChanged="OnLanguageChanged" />

    <TextBlock Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="AppearanceLabel" VerticalAlignment="Center"
               Margin="0,7,0,0" />
    <ComboBox Grid.Row="1" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="AppearanceCombo" Width="220"
              HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,7,0,0"
              AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #AppearanceLabel}"
              SelectionChanged="OnAppearanceChanged" />

    <TextBlock Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="FontLabel" VerticalAlignment="Center"
               Margin="0,7,0,0" />
    <!-- IsTextSearchEnabled restores the WPF list's type-ahead: with hundreds of installed
         families, typing the first letters is how the list is actually navigated. The rows are
         ComboBoxItems whose Content is the display name, so no TextSearch.TextPath is needed, and
         the disabled group-header rows are skipped because they are not selectable. -->
    <ComboBox Grid.Row="2" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="FontCombo" Width="220" MaxDropDownHeight="320"
              IsTextSearchEnabled="True" HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center"
              Margin="0,7,0,0"
              AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #FontLabel}"
              SelectionChanged="OnFontChanged" />

    <!-- Round 3, Task 4: replaces the removed 按钮大小 (小/中/大) row. Nothing on this surface names
         a tile width any more — Task 4 fixed every tile at the width that row had called "小"
         (168 DIP), and Round 4, item 3 removed that fixed width too, so a tile is as wide as its own
         icon-plus-label content (see GeneralConfig.ButtonSize's remarks, and TmTileButton in
         Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml, which carries no Width Setter). This row governs how many
         shortcuts a column holds before wrapping, not how big a tile is. A NumericUpDown, not a
         ComboBox like the other rows here: the value is an integer the user picks from a small
         range, not one of a short list of named choices, and Avalonia ships NumericUpDown as a plain
         control (no extra NuGet package) that already gives a spinner AND type-in for exactly that.
         Range 1–30 matches the pre-Avalonia Blazor build's own numeric input for this same setting
         (docs/plans/2026-06-22-tanmenu-wpf-blazor.md: `min="1" max="30"`) — below 1 a "column" is not
         a column, and 30 comfortably covers even a large 常用工具/folder group without inviting a
         single, absurdly tall column. ClipValueToMinMax keeps a typed out-of-range value inside that
         band rather than accepting it verbatim. -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="0" x:Name="ColCountLabel" VerticalAlignment="Center"
               Margin="0,7,0,0" />
    <NumericUpDown Grid.Row="3" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="ColCountUpDown" Width="220"
                   HorizontalAlignment="Left" VerticalAlignment="Center" Margin="0,7,0,0"
                   Minimum="1" Maximum="30" Increment="1" FormatString="0"
                   ClipValueToMinMax="True"
                   AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #ColCountLabel}"
                   ValueChanged="OnColCountChanged" />

    <!-- 3 is PUBLISHED on the same page: "the smallest space between a non-interactive control and
         any other control". This line is static text describing the spinner directly above it, not a
         new row, so it sits one rung below the 7 that separates two rows. -->
    <TextBlock Grid.Row="4" Grid.Column="1" x:Name="ColCountHelp" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Margin="0,3,0,0" />
  </Grid>
</UserControl>
